                    <death when="1000"/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">The identification of '2 Landseers' or '3 Landseers' is difficult to deduce from the diary. Godwin seems to have similar levels of contact
    with both <persName key="LAN02">John Landseer</persName> and his sons, <persName key="LAN07">Charles</persName> and <persName key="LAN08">Edwin</persName> .  
    It may be that '2 Landseers' often refer to the brothers and 
    '3 Landseers' to the trio. An 1836 entry refers to a sister however, and the Landseer family was sufficiently large and talented (see <title>DNB</title>) to make any such statements 
    tenuous.  Indeed, the entry of 10 October 1831 (Sup at Martin’s, w. Edwin + 3 Landseers) disproves it as a general rule.  
  </note>
                    <bibl type="other"><title>DNB</title>&gt;</bibl>
                </person>
